comments.body:
@participant8 Yes, might be off topic, but it's an emergency situation...

Thanks for your guide. It works. I have done it for one of my three meters, now (two to go). 

Bear in mind, if anyone else is following @participant8's guide: If you start just before the hour (correcting several days) and don't finish until after the hour, you may end up getting twice the correction on the last hourly entry in the statistics table. (I did that and had to subtract it again when I discovered it.)

@participant5 https://<redacted-host>/blog/

2021.11 "A new state class, total has been added and the last_reset attribute has been added back to SensorEntity and is no longer deprecated."

So I guess it's fine now?

> I can help test this. Do you know if there is a guide for testing? Or is it as simple as cloning "tibber" folder from the bugfix branch https://github.com/home-assistant/core/tree/tibber_bug_61551/homeassistant/components/tibber into custom_components/ followed by a restart of HA core? I am running HA OS.

I just patched the right file manually. The file resides here (at least on my installation):
`/usr/src/homeassistant/homeassistant/components/tibber/sensor.py`

I used `vi` to edit the file and inserted the below lines in the right spot, please see [https://github.com/home-assistant/core/pull/61914/files](https://github.com/home-assistant/core/pull/61914/files)

```
        if self.entity_description.key in (
            "accumulatedConsumption",
            "accumulatedProduction",
        ):
            # Value is reset to 0 at midnight, but not always strictly increasing due to hourly corrections
            ts_local = dt_util.parse_datetime(live_measurement["timestamp"])
            if ts_local is not None:
                if self.last_reset is None or (
                    self.native_value > state and ts_local.hour == 0
                ):
                    self._attr_last_reset = dt_util.as_utc(
                        ts_local.replace(hour=0, minute=0, second=0, microsecond=0)
                    )
```

It seems that only the first hour after patching gets the huge consumption shown in [this comment](https://github.com/home-assistant/core/issues/61551#issuecomment-995788346), the next hours show expected consumptions. The really interesting thing will be to see how the component behaves around midnight.

@participant13 Energy dashboard's value for e.g. 13-14 will be the change in the sensor that occurred between 13:00 and 14:00. If the sensor changes value 14:00:10, that change will be counted for hour 14-15. AFAIK there is no way for any integration to add statistics data with a timestamp that has already passed - although that would be the 'correct' solution, giving the energy dashboard exactly same values as the power company uses for the bill. If you'd like that, I suggest adding that as a separate feature request.

> ... last_meter_consumption has the same issue as accumulated_consumption_xxx with the 1 hour offset

I do not experience any 1 hour offset-issues with accumulated_consumption. Have you tested the fix and still seeing a 1 hour offset for **accumulated_consumption_xxx**? If so, might it be a separate issue with e.g. timezone or such?

@participant10 you don’t have an offset with the total meter sensor?
I have Oslo time zone both in HA and in Tibber API

I did not apply the fix yay but it looks like it only applies to the resetting daily sensor
